app tar-spec [
  version: "1.0.0",
  description: "Design contract for unpacking a tar archive (POSIX ustar, with PAX path records) whose bytes are already decompressed, the step after std-gzip in every package install. TAR_UNPACK { data } answers TAR_ANSWERED { files } with one row { path, kind, mode, size, data : bytes } per regular file (typeflag 0 or NUL, kind file) and per directory (typeflag 5, kind directory, size 0, empty data); consumers read kind, never the shape of the path, in archive order, and TAR_REFUSED { reason, entry } otherwise, never a partial result. A header is 512 bytes: name (100 bytes, to the first NUL) joined to the ustar prefix (155 bytes at offset 345, only when the magic at offset 257 is ustar) with a slash; mode and size are octal text; the checksum is the sum of the 512 header bytes with the 8 checksum bytes counted as spaces. File data follows, padded to 512; the archive ends at two all-zero blocks. A PAX x header (typeflag x) carries records of the form 'len key=value newline'; its path record replaces the path of the next entry, and its size record replaces the size in the next entry header (the way PAX carries sizes beyond the 11-digit octal limit); both apply to that entry only. The keys atime, mtime, ctime, uid, gid, uname, gname, charset, hdrcharset, comment and linkpath are metadata that does not change what bytes the entry unpacks to (linkpath only means something to the link typeflags, which are refused on their own) and are ignored; every other key, vendor extensions included, is refused as pax-key with entry set to the key. Refusal reasons are closed: truncated (a header or its data runs past the bytes, or the two end blocks are missing), octal (a size, mode or checksum field is not octal text), checksum, end-marker (a zero block followed by a non-zero one), pax (a malformed PAX record or size), pax-key (an unknown non-metadata PAX key), unsafe-path (the entry path is empty, absolute, or has a .. segment), and one named reason per unsupported typeflag, never skipped: symlink (2), hardlink (1), device (3 or 4), gnu-long-name (L or K), pax-global (g), typeflag (anything else). entry is the path of the refused entry. Golden vectors from system tar --format=ustar. Two files: a.txt (hello newline, mode 0644) and pkg/b.json (the 8 bytes {\"n\":1} newline), 3072 bytes,  answers two rows, a.txt size 6 mode 420 data aGVsbG8K and pkg/b.json size 8 mode 420. PAX path: an x header whose record is path=long/dir/renamed.txt before an entry named short.txt holding hello newline, 3072 bytes,  answers one row at long/dir/renamed.txt, kind file. Directory: one entry pkg/ of typeflag 5, 1536 bytes,  answers one row at pkg/ with kind directory and size 0. Symlink: one entry link of typeflag 2 pointing at a.txt, 1536 bytes,  refuses symlink with entry link. PAX size: an x header with records path=big/size.txt, size=6, mtime=1.5 and uid=0 before an entry whose own header size is 0 followed by hello newline, 3072 bytes,  answers one row at big/size.txt with size 6 and data aGVsbG8K. PAX unknown key: an x header with record FOO.bar=x, 3072 bytes,  refuses pax-key with entry FOO.bar. The same two-file archive cut to its first 1000 bytes refuses truncated; with one byte of the first header changed refuses checksum. Values it replaces: the untar step of every package install."
]

type TarFile = { path : string!, kind : "file" | "directory", mode : number!, size : number!, data : bytes! }

orbital TarSpec {
  expects entity TarRun [runtime] {
    id : string!
  }
  expects trait Untar {
    state idle {
      TAR_UNPACK -> idle
    }
    emits {
      TAR_ANSWERED { files : [TarFile]! }
      TAR_REFUSED { reason : string!  entry : string! }
    }
  }
  expects page "/tar" -> Untar
}
